<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<appendix xml:id="appa-units">
	<title>Units and Unit Conversions</title>

	<introduction>

		<p>When describing a physical property, a unit of measure is typically required. A unit gives a sense of perspective or scale relating to the magnitude of the value. Simply stating that the current through a circuit branch is 10 is incomplete. Is the unit amps or milliamps? The difference could be dangerous or even harmful to someone interacting with the circuit.</p>
		
		<p>It is important to note that two quantities with different units cannot be combined together using addition or subtraction. (A common phrase used by physics and engineering professors is that <q>you can't add apples to oranges.</q>) This saying is helpful to keep in mind when performing a <q>smell test</q> on calculations. If an addition or subtraction operation is being performed between two or more quantities, then those quantities <alert>must</alert> have the same unit. If not, a mistake was made at some point in the problem-solving process. (This is particularly helpful when deriving differential equations in <xref ref="ch7-secondorder" /> or transfer functions in <xref ref="ch9-phasoranalysis" /> or <xref ref="ch11-laplace" />.)</p>

		<p>It is strongly recommended that every student perform a dimensional analysis on problems prior to solving. In this book, questions in later chapters frequently utilize base units (which are units used without an SI prefix, not necessarily to be confused with <xref ref="appa-si-base-units" />) to ensure that the dimensional analysis will work out properly. This will be explored in a little more detail in <xref ref="appa-diffeq-coefficients" />.</p>
		
		<p>It is expected that the reader of this textbook is familiar with the unit conversion process. It will not be covered in this appendix.</p>

		<remark>
			<p>Portions of this chapter of this textbook were adapted from <url href="https://cod.pressbooks.pub/physics1100/">Conceptual Physics</url> by Alyssa J. Pasquale, Ph.D., David R. Fazzini, Ph.D., and Carley Bennett, Ph.D. This book is licensed under <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">CC BY-NC 4.0</url>.</p>
		</remark>
  	</introduction>

  	<section xml:id="appa-section1">
		<title>The International Standard of Units</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>The <term>international standard of units (SI)</term> is the modern system of units. It is considered a <term>metric system</term> as it is composed of units that are based on powers of ten. This system of units uses base units (described in <xref ref="appa-si-base-units-table" />) that are explained by physical properties such as the speed of light in a vacuum (a value that does not change regardless of one's position or motion in the universe). The SI system of units is the established standard used in scientific fields (even in the United States).</p>
		
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="appa-si-base-units">
			<title>SI Base Units</title>
				
			<p>There are seven units in the SI system that can be used to derive every other unit in the system. These seven units are known as SI base units. The SI base units are defined in <xref ref="appa-si-base-units-table" />.</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Note on the use of the term <q>base units</q></title>

				<p>Problems worked out in this textbook will frequently use the term <q>base units</q> to describe a unit used without an SI prefix (A instead of mA, for example). This should not be confused with SI base units!</p>
			</remark>

			<table xml:id="appa-si-base-units-table">
				<title>SI Base Units.</title>
  				<tabular halign="left" width="100%">
					<col width="20%" />
					<col width="30%" />
					<col width="50%" />
    				<row header="yes" bottom="medium">
      					<cell><p>Symbol</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>Name</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>Measurement</p></cell>
	    			</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>s</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>second</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>time</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>m</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>meter</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>length</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>kg</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>kilogram</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>mass</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>A</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>ampere</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>electrical current</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>K</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>Kelvin</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>temperature</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>mol</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>mole</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>quantity of a substance</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>cd</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>candela</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>luminous intensity</p></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="appa-si-derived-units">
			<title>SI Derived Units</title>
				
			<p>All other units can be derived from base units and are therefore called derived units. For example, the unit used for velocity (meters per second) is derived from the base units of meter and second: m/s. Most of the units used in this book are derived units. These will be presented in <xref ref="appa-table-of-units-in-this-book" /> in <xref ref="appa-section2" /> for the units used in this textbook.</p>
		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="appa-si-prefixes">
			<title>SI Prefixes</title>
				
			<p>At times, there are quantities that are either extremely small or extremely large. Instead of using scientific notation to deal with these quantities, we can instead use prefixes. The <term>SI prefixes</term> have been standardized for use with SI units. These prefixes can be used with any type of SI unit: meter, volt, second, gram, and so on. Common SI prefixes are defined in <xref ref="appa-table-of-si-prefixes" />.</p>

			<table xml:id="appa-table-of-si-prefixes">
