		<subsection xml:id="appa-diffeq-coefficients">
			<title>Units for Coefficients in Differential Equations and Transfer Functions</title>

			<p>It can be difficult to understand the units involved with quantities used to derive the coefficients of a differential equation. (These coefficients are introduced in <xref ref="ch5-first-order-linear-ODEs" /> for first-order circuits and in <xref ref="ch7-second-order-review" /> for second-order circuits.)</p>
			
			<p>In first-order differential equations, the unit of each term in the equation will either be V/s or A/s. This means that <m>y(t)</m> will have a unit of V/s or A/s, <m>a_1</m> (which is equal to one in a standard-form differential equation) will have no unit, and <m>a_0</m> will have a unit of 1/s.</p>
			
			<p>In first-order RC circuits, the coefficient <m>a_0</m> has an equation in terms of <m>\frac{1}{RC}</m>. The unit derivation, demonstrating that the unit is 1/s, is shown in <xref ref="appa-rc-a0" />. In non-homogeneous RC circuits, when solving for voltage, the function <m>y(t)</m> has an equation in terms of <m>\frac{V}{RC}</m>, which has a unit of V/s.</p>
			
			<proof xml:id="appa-rc-a0">
				<p>
					<md>\frac{1}{\textrm{F}\Omega} = \frac{1}{\left(\frac{\textrm{s}^4\textrm{A}^2}{\textrm{kgm}^2}\right)\left(\frac{\textrm{kgm}^2}{\textrm{s}^3\textrm{A}^2}\right)} = \frac{1}{\textrm{s}}</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>In first-order RL circuits, the coefficient <m>a_0</m> has an equation in terms of <m>\frac{R}{L}</m>. The unit derivation, demonstrating that the unit is 1/s, is shown in <xref ref="appa-rl-a0" />. In non-homogeneous RL circuits, when solving for current, the function <m>y(t)</m> has an equation in terms of <m>\frac{IR}{L}</m>, which has a unit of A/s.</p>

			<proof xml:id="appa-rl-a0">
				<p>
					<md>\frac{\Omega}{\textrm{H}} = \frac{\frac{\textrm{kgm}^2}{\textrm{s}^3\textrm{A}^2}}{\frac{\textrm{kgm}^2}{\textrm{s}^2\textrm{A}^2}} = \frac{1}{\textrm{s}}</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>In second-order differential equations, the unit of each term in the equation will either be V/s<m>^2</m> or A/s<m>^2</m>. This means that <m>y(t)</m> will have a unit of V/s<m>^2</m> or A/s<m>^2</m>, <m>a_2</m> (which is equal to one in a standard-form differential equation) will have no unit, <m>a_1</m> will have a unit of 1/s, and <m>a_0</m> will have a unit of 1/s<m>^2</m>.</p>

			<p>In second-order circuits, the coefficient <m>a_1</m> will have an equation in terms of <m>\frac{1}{RC}</m> and <m>\frac{R}{L}</m>, which were demonstrated in <xref ref="appa-rc-a0" /> and <xref ref="appa-rl-a0" /> to have a unit of 1/s. The coefficient <m>a_0</m> will have an equation in terms of <m>\frac{1}{LC}</m>. The unit derivation, demonstrating that the unit is 1/s<m>^2</m>, is shown in <xref ref="appa-rlc-a0" />. In non-homogeneous second-order circuits, the function <m>y(t)</m> has an equation in terms of <m>\frac{V}{LC}</m>, which has a unit of V/s<m>^2</m>, <m>\frac{IR}{LC}</m> which has a unit of V/s<m>^2</m>, <m>\frac{V}{RLC}</m>, which has a unit of A/s<m>^2</m>, or <m>\frac{I}{LC}</m>, which has a unit of A/s<m>^2</m>.</p>

			<proof xml:id="appa-rlc-a0">
				<p>
					<md>\frac{1}{\textrm{FH}} = \frac{1}{\left(\frac{\textrm{s}^4\textrm{A}^2}{\textrm{kgm}^2}\right)\left(\frac{\textrm{kgm}^2}{\textrm{s}^2\textrm{A}^2}\right)} = \frac{1}{\textrm{s}^2}</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>The damping parameter, <m>\alpha</m> has a unit of Np/s. In SI base units, that is a unit of 1/s. This is consistent with the unit derivation shown in <xref ref="appa-rc-a0" /> and <xref ref="appa-rl-a0" />. The resonant frequency, <m>\omega_0</m>, has a unit of rad/s. In SI base units, that is a unit of 1/s. The derivation for this unit is shown in <xref ref="appa-rlc-w0" />.</p>
			
			<proof xml:id="appa-rlc-w0">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{a_0}</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{\textrm{s}} \amp= \sqrt{\frac{1}{\textrm{s}^2}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{\textrm{s}}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>
		</subsection>

	</section>

</appendix>